# =============================================================================
# cape — A panic at the end of a do body is fatal and the catch cannot run.
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • `panic` is fatal, not part of the `catch` channel — a `do` body that ends
#     in `panic` aborts the process; its trailing `catch` handler is unreachable
#   • Body prefix still runs — statements before the `panic` execute and print
#     before the run ends on the trap
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • Expecting `catch` to recover a `panic` — only `throw`/`iace` rides the
#     recovery channel; use `throw` when a handler must intercept the failure
#
# See also: cape, mori, fac
# =============================================================================

main {
    do {
        print "in"
        panic "x"
    }
    catch e {
        print "caught"
    }
}
